# A declaration is read at its own site.  The method `first` of this source and the method `part_last` of the program part
# declaration_site_part.lm2 -- the last method of the unit -- each declare a pointer place `p` with its type and its value.
# The declaration of `first` stands further from the start of its file than the one of the part does from the start of
# the part.  A translator that read the declarations of every method under the method an earlier pass had left current
# took the place `p` of `part_last` for the place `@: p int @ x` repeats and refused the statement: "a declaration takes
# one value".  Each method reads through its own place.  Entry 7; 11 and 12 name the failing check.
fn: first () int
    int: x 5
    @: p int @ x
    return: \p
end: first
int: code 7
if: first() != 5
    code := 11
if: code = 7 && part_last() != 2
    code := 12
send: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
